This was based on the 3.15 PR: https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/pull/43129/files

Fixes issue dotnet/runtime#57472

Main pr dotnet/runtime#57479

Also backported to 5.0 in dotnet/runtime#57562 and 3.1 in #43102. This PR will pave the way for backporting it to the `System.Memory` OOB package for .NET Framework.

# Customer Impact

A customer has encountered dotnet/runtime#57472 while on .NET Framework and reported it in dotnet/runtime#67295, necessiating backporting it once again. As an example provided in that issue, this memory manager:

``` csharp
private sealed class BasicMemoryManager : MemoryManager<byte>
{
    private readonly byte[] _buffer;
    public BasicMemoryManager(int size = 1024) => _buffer = new byte[size];

    protected override bool TryGetArray(out ArraySegment<byte> segment)
    {
        segment = new ArraySegment<byte>(_buffer);
        return true;
    }
    public override Span<byte> GetSpan() => _buffer;

    public override MemoryHandle Pin(int elementIndex = 0) => throw new NotSupportedException();
    public override void Unpin() => throw new NotSupportedException();
    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing) { }
}
```

will fail two of the following tests:

``` csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 32)]
[InlineData(0, 29)]
[InlineData(32, 32)] // fail: length 0
[InlineData(32, 29)] // fail: length -3
public void OffsetTest(int offset, int length)
{
    var mgr = new BasicMemoryManager(1024);
    var slice = mgr.Memory.Slice(offset, length);
    Assert.Equal(length, slice.Length);

    var ros = new ReadOnlySequence<byte>(slice);
    Assert.True(ros.IsSingleSegment);
    Assert.Equal(length, ros.Length);
}
```

# Testing

Failing test has been added in this PR.

# Risk

Low, as the change is very simple and obvious when you look at `ReadOnlySequence<T>` ctor that accepts `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` (notice line 133 versus lines 140 and 148):

https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/blob/0d40b482e6b6c232131dc808182d6b32d9d37394/src/System.Memory/src/System/Buffers/ReadOnlySequence.cs#L130-L149

Moreover, the changed code gets triggered only when creating a `ReadOnlySequence` from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by a `MemoryManager`. All other use cases (including creating from an array or from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by an array or a string) are unaffected. And the changes are identical to those in the other backports.

# Regression

This is not a regression.

LSP go-to-definition and go-to-type-definition can return an old source offset after another open document changes. The offset is converted using the current document text, so the resulting line/column points at the wrong characters.

Reproduced on current main `ff2e51158d0f18bf40381246097f2a5dbd83118e`, with a locally built standalone server, and on NuGet `roslyn-language-server` 5.13.0-1.26514.10 (macOS arm64).

Minimal case:

```csharp
// A.cs
class A { public static void M() { } }
// B.cs
class B { void Use() { A.M(); } }
```

Open both documents, request the definition of `M` in `B.cs`, insert a comment line at the start of `A.cs` with `textDocument/didChange`, and request the definition from unchanged `B.cs` again. The result should identify `M` at its new position. Instead it can retain the old offset. A fresh server on the edited files returns the correct location. Inserting spaces on the same line also reproduces the problem.

A regression in `GoToDefinitionTests` reproduces all eight combinations of definition/type-definition, line/column movement, and mutating/non-mutating LSP workspaces. It is necessary to set `IWorkspacePartialSolutionsTestHook.IsPartialSolutionDisabled = false`: production enables partial semantics, but test workspaces disable them by default. All eight cases fail without a fix.

The likely cause is `AbstractNavigableItemsService` accepting a symbol from a frozen snapshot whose target tree is old, while the LSP handler resolves its `NavigableDocument` ID in the current solution. The optimization from #72714 is valuable; a proposed narrow fix preserves it when source locations use current trees and retries full semantics only for stale targets.

A separate [21-site probe](https://github.com/julymetodiev/lantern-lattice/pull/2) reproduces 12 wrong target columns after declaration movement on current main. This affects a compiler-backed call graph: caching these locations can link the wrong symbols. The probe itself is client-side diagnostic evidence, separate from the failing Roslyn regression.

